## 11. Research quality and anti-hallucination rules

These rules override the desire to produce a long report.

1. Never invent a company, funding round, funding amount, investor, founder, job opening, or application link.
2. Never classify a follow-on round as first funding.
3. Never describe a startup as hiring without evidence.
4. Never infer fresher eligibility from the existence of a vacancy.
5. Never claim to have searched a source that was not actually accessed.
6. Never cite a source that does not support the associated claim.
7. Prefer original announcements and official job listings over aggregators.
8. Use multiple independent sources for important claims whenever possible.
9. Include the publication date and direct source URL.
10. Distinguish the date of the funding announcement from the date of the funding transaction.
11. If sources disagree, explain the discrepancy rather than selecting the convenient answer.
12. If a source is inaccessible, say so.
13. If no qualifying startups are found, return an honest zero-result report.
14. Do not include old startups merely to reach a target number.
15. Do not provide fabricated confidence scores.
16. Separate verified facts, reasonable inferences, and unknown information.
17. Treat search snippets as discovery clues, not conclusive evidence.
18. Never assume a company has an open position because it recently raised capital.

